# Calling a continuation in tail position keeps a stack frame, so a loop
# written in continuation-passing style runs out of stack.
#
# Run from this directory: kanso run main.kso --interp (stack diagnostic);
#  kanso build main.kso && ./main (segfault)
#
# Expected: `cps 100000` prints 5000050000 on every engine. Appendix A says "a
# tail call costs no stack at all", and `then (acc + i)` and the lambda's
# `loop next (i + 1) n` are both in tail position.
# Actual: the interpreter stops at 10,000 steps with "error[runtime]: the
# program ran out of stack"; the dev build prints the answer at 10,000 and
# dies with a segmentation fault (exit 139) at 100,000, where the interpreter
# gives the diagnostic. So the engines also disagree about how they fail.
#
# Where it bit: continuation-passing was the way to keep `put` in place in
# compiled code (a `put` passed straight into a tail call does not copy), so
# the TOML decoder's insertion steps took a `then`. A 300 KB document then
# overflowed the interpreter's stack.
fn loop acc i n
  return acc if n < i
  step acc i (next -> loop next (i + 1) n)

fn step acc i then
  then (acc + i)

pub fn cps n
  loop 0 1 n
